 ],
 "caption": "Nothing in the jar changed. Only the clock did.\n\nIn August, Jif announced its first major design update in more than 30 years. The tri-colour banner is back, the drop shadow is gone, but the real shift is on the label: new images show Jif with apple slices, crackers and rice cakes, or straight off a spoon. Jif To Go is now Jif Dippers, and there are new squeezable formats. The company says the product itself isn't changing.\n\nMy take: before you build a new product, look at the moments of your customer's day where you don't show up yet. The Ehrenberg-Bass Institute (Jenni Romaniuk) calls these category entry points, and they add a warning: more moments don't automatically mean more growth. You have to pick the right ones.\n\nThe limits: the new packs only started shipping in October 2026 and no sales results have been published, so we don't know yet whether it works. Snacking figures in the coverage (like the Spins 56% cited by FoodNavigator) are second-hand; I haven't read the original reports. Sources: the J.M. Smucker press release, MediaPost, FoodNavigator. Photos: Jif / J.M. Smucker. Other objects are AI-generated.\n\nWhat are the three moments people reach for you, and what's the fourth one nobody owns yet? Write to me.\n\n— Behnood Kavian",
